# NIST AI RMF (2023 + 2024)

**Full citation (1.0):** NIST. (2023). *Artificial Intelligence Risk Management Framework (AI RMF 1.0)* (NIST AI 100-1). National Institute of Standards and Technology, U.S. Department of Commerce. https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.AI.100-1  
**Full citation (GPAI Profile):** NIST. (2024). *Artificial Intelligence Risk Management Framework: Generative Artificial Intelligence Profile* (NIST AI 600-1). National Institute of Standards and Technology, U.S. Department of Commerce. https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.AI.600-1  
**Zotero key:** `nist_ai_rmf_2023` / `nist_ai_rmf_gpai_2024`

---

## Core Argument

The AI RMF 1.0 provides a voluntary, enterprise-facing risk management framework organised around four functions — Govern, Map, Measure, Manage — applicable across the AI system lifecycle. It is non-binding, organisation-facing, and agnostic about enforcement. The GPAI Profile (NIST AI 600-1) extends the framework to foundation model and generative AI risks. Both treat accountability as an organisational rather than institutional construct — they do not address answerability to named statutory authorities for proxy-to-mandate translations.

---

## Why This Work Matters to PRIS

The NIST AI RMF is simultaneously a **framework case** in the fsQCA corpus, a **comparative benchmark** in P4's international comparator section, and a **gap-defining reference** in P5 (the RMF underspecifies institutional answerability in the public administration context). It is also the most practically influential US AI governance document and the primary reference for the AI Governance Wiki's US coverage.

