## How Each Paper Uses It

| Paper | Section | Specific Use | What the argument depends on |
|---|---|---|---|
| P4 | §2 (International benchmarks) | NIST AI RMF as the US voluntary-standard comparator — illustrates what India lacks: a cross-sectoral voluntary standard operationalising enterprise-level governance roles | Benchmark function — shows the gap in Indian governance architecture |
| P4 | §5 (Redesign proposal) | The Govern function's role-specification architecture is a design reference for the enterprise-level layer of P4's Three-Level Governance Alignment Model | Governance function as a design anchor |
| P5 | §1.4 (AI governance frameworks) | NIST AI RMF is one of four governance frameworks P5 positions its contribution as extending — the gap P5 fills is the underspecification of institutional answerability in the public administration delegation context | "Underspecifies institutional answerability" — precise gap claim |
| P5 | §4 (Three-phase architecture) | The three-phase architecture is positioned as an extension of the AI RMF for the public-administration delegation context | Extension, not replacement |
| fsQCA Chapter | §3 (RL condition); §6 (calibration) | Expected high SR, low RL — voluntary with detailed role specification; calibration pending Q3 2026 coding | The SR-high/RL-low profile may generate a distinctive configuration in the truth table |

